            "context_tokens": 32768
          }
        },
        "dense": {
          "answer": "The function $\\beta(r)$ is determined by applying the Einstein vacuum equations, specifically the Baez-Bunn form, to a test ball in free fall alongside one of the family of infalling observers. This leads to a differential equation for $\\beta(r)$ that can be solved using the method of separation of variables, ultimately yielding two integration constants. The values of these constants are fixed by the initial condition for the infalling observers and the Newtonian limit at large distances from the center of symmetry.",
          "found": true,
          "f1": 0.18421052631578946,
          "exact_match": 0.0,
          "metrics": {
            "elapsed_seconds": 22.17675875002169,
            "generation_seconds": 19.583234874997288,
            "embedding_seconds": 2.5888862080173567,
            "generation_calls": 1,
            "embedding_requests": 3,
            "embedded_texts": 25,
            "embedding_cache_hits": 0,
            "prompt_tokens": 3817,
            "output_tokens": 101,
            "input_words": 2000,
            "context_tokens": 32768
          }
        },
        "direct": {
          "answer": "The function beta(r) is determined by applying the vacuum Einstein equations, specifically equation (\\ref{EinsteinVacuum}), to a test ball in free fall alongside one of the family of infalling observers. This leads to the differential equation:\n\n\u03b2(r)\u03b2'(r) = C/r^2\n\nwhere C is an integration constant.\n\nThe value of C is determined by using the initial condition for the infalling observers and the Newtonian limit at large distances from the center of symmetry. Specifically, it is set equal to -GM/c^2, where GM is the gravitational constant and c is the speed of light.\n\nBy solving this differential equation using the method of separation of variables, we find that beta(r) can be expressed as:\n\n\u03b2(r) = \u221a(-2GM/r + D)\n\nwhere D is another integration constant. Setting D = 0 gives the final form of beta(r):\n\n\u03b2(r) = \u221a(2GM/r)\n\nThis result is used to transform the metric into the traditional Schwarzschild form.",
          "found": true,
          "f1": 0.09022556390977443,
          "exact_match": 0.0,
